/-!
# Condition (2) of Tag 0CBR implies strict normal crossings, given a closed branch cover

Let `E` be a divisor on a locally Noetherian scheme `U` satisfying condition (2) of Tag 0CBR,
written `Stacks.Divisors.IsNormalizationCrossingsDivisor`, and suppose moreover that the
normalization `D^ν` of the subscheme cut out by `E` is a finite disjoint union of open subschemes
`A j`, each of which maps to `U` by a closed immersion. Then `E` is a strict normal crossings
divisor.

This is the hard half of the implication from condition (2) to condition (1) of Tag 0CBR: the
étale-local splitting of the normalization supplied by Tag 04HJ puts one in exactly the situation
described above, and what is proved here is that the local equation of `E` at a point is then the
product of a nonempty initial segment of a regular system of parameters.

## Main results

* `isStrictNormalCrossingsDivisor_of_isNormalizationCrossingsDivisor_of_closedCover`: the
  statement above.

## Implementation notes

Everything happens in the local ring `R = 𝒪_{U, x}` at a point `x` of the support of `E`. Write
`ν₀` for the normalization of the subscheme cut out by `E`, `ν` for its composition with the
closed immersion of that subscheme, and `I j` for the kernel ideal sheaf of `ε j ≫ ν`, the
`j`-th *branch ideal*. The branches through `x` are the `j` with `x` in the support of `I j`, and
the whole proof is the computation of the stalk of `E` at `x` as the product of one local
equation for each of them.

The single geometric input is the open cover of the complement of the fat diagonal in a fibre
power by pieces isomorphic over `U` to the subschemes cut out by the finite suprema
`⨆ k, I (t k)` along injective tuples `t`, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_tupleOpens_cover_fibrePowerDistinctOpens`. Transporting the three
clauses of condition (2) at `n = r` across that cover gives, for every injective `r`-tuple `t`,
that the closed immersion of the subscheme cut out by `⨆ k, I (t k)` is a local complete
intersection morphism with regular local rings on the source and with every stalk ideal needing
exactly `r` generators. Regularity of the source is transported pointwise, as regularity of a
stalk, rather than as a property of a scheme; the conormal rank is transported as the minimal
number of generators of a stalk ideal, which needs the conormal sheaf of the restriction to an
open (Tag 04FA) and the flat base change of Tag 04F9 along the isomorphism supplied by the cover,
because the morphism `Z_r ⟶ U` is itself not an immersion and so has no stalk-ideal description.

Being a local complete intersection morphism and a closed immersion, that morphism is
Koszul-regular by Tag 069G, hence regular by Tag 063L, so each of its stalk ideals is generated by
a regular sequence; since the minimal number of generators is `r`, that sequence has length `r`,
and any other generating family of `r` elements is then a regular sequence as well. Applied with
`r` the number of branches through `x` this produces a regular sequence of local equations of the
branches, and applied with `r = 1` it produces one local equation `g j` of each single branch.

That the product of those equations is exactly the stalk of `E` is two inclusions. One is formal:
`E` is below every branch ideal, so its stalk is inside every principal ideal `(g j)` and hence
inside their product, the sequence being regular. The other is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.stalk_eq_iInf_of_finite_cover`, which says that the
stalk of `E` is the infimum of the stalks of the branch ideals; the branches not through `x`
contribute the unit ideal, and each branch through `x` contributes a principal ideal containing
the product.

Finally the regular sequence of branch equations through `x` cuts out a regular local ring,
because the subscheme it defines is one of those transported from the cover, so by Stacks 00NU the
ring `R` itself is regular and the sequence extends to a regular system of parameters whose
initial segment it is.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CBR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CBR)
